Question 2:

a. What is the key difference between AWS Lambda and AWS EC2?
AWS EC2 is Amazon’s scalable, on-demand VM (Virtual Machine) solution. AWS maintains the  hardware but the OS, disks, processes, etc. are all your responsibility.
AWS Lambda is AWS’s function execution solution. You bring a function that you want executed and a definition of the trigger. AWS takes care of the rest

b. What are the two key differences between AWS Lambda and AWS spot
instances (think: pricing and how long instances last)?
AWS Spot is Amazon offering discounted VMs for leftover capacity. Billing is like EC2, you pay for the VM including when idle. No time bound cap.
AWS Lambda persists for the duration of one execution with a time bound cap. You are billed per invocation plus seconds of runtime.

c. What is the key difference between a “regular” AWS instance (EC2) and a
“burstable” AWS instance?
“Regular” AWS EC2 instance: you are reserving a full CPU at the full rate, indefinitely.
In a burstable AWS instance, you reserve a small portion of a CPU. If you run under that you accumulate credits, if you run the CPU over that you burn credits or billed.

d. What is the difference between a GPU and a TPU (among cloud
offerings)?
GPUs and TPUs are both chips that you can attach to a VM in cloud environments. GPUs are general-purpose parallel processors optimized for a variety of tasks. TPUs are built specifically for AI workloads, specifically tensor math/matrix multiplication. TPUs are optimized for high-throughput deep learning.

e. What is the difference between AWS spot instances and Google Cloud
preemptible instances?
Both architectures are the public cloud providers selling leftover VM capacity at a discount.
AWS spot has no time bound cap. AWS will take the capacity back from you at any time. GCP preemptible has a 24-hour hard cap where Google will take that capacity back no matter what.

f. Give one example application (class) where you would prefer AWS EC2,
and one where you would prefer AWS Lambda. Justify your choices
briefly.
AWS EC2 preferred when you are needing an application API that is always accessible. This is because EC2 VMs are long running and are load ready, and allow for granular control of things like the OS and in-memory processes. AWS Lambda is preferred when you need to do something like a simple parse of a JSON or ping a webhook. Lambda specialize in often zero functions that are short and bursty.








Question 3:

a. Define each of these 6 terms concisely

Container: A stand-alone and encapsulated process or application that is isolated from the other processes on the host.
Docker: Docker is a virtualization platform that operates at the OS-level and allows different containers to operate on the same kernel.
Virtual Machine: A computer emulation including the Operating System, CPU/RAM/Disk/GPU, kernel, etc. but with hypervisor in play for isolation from the host machine.
Kubernetes: An open source container orchestrator which is used to schedule, restart, and manage containers across multiple machines.
K8 pod: The smallest unit that K8 manages, one or more containers with shared namespace and orchestration.
K8 Cluster: A whole K8 control plane with different types of servers, multiple pods, and worker nodes.

b. Among the four possibilities of docker, container, VM, and K8s, give one
scenario where the fencing team would use each of them over the other
three possibilities. Think of applications. (Keep your answer to < 100
words).

VM: If the fencing team is running an application which requires a full OS and kernel, such as a vendor analysis program, a VM is likely the right tool.
Container: The fencing team should reach for a container when they need a locked down and isolated process such as a long running python process from an opponent team.
Docker: Docker is vital when you need to maange a repeatable container image, such as when the coach has a docherfile + app to run simulated game instances.
K8s: Let’s say the team is doing a multi day tournament where they need a separate but centrally managed containers for running game simulations, they would need K8s.

c. Give two key differences between a K8s node, K8s pod, and K8s cluster.
A K8s node, pod, and cluster are all describing different scopes/sizes of the system. A node is a single machine (VM or physical server), a Pod is a collection of containers, which is where the scheduling/management takes place, and a Cluster is the entire K8 system. A Cluster contains many nodes, and a node runs many pods. When a pod fails K8s spins up a new one, when a node fails all pods on that node fail, when a cluster goes down it all goes down.













