Question 6:
To train new sportspersons, US sports agencies have been wondering whether it
is faster to “push” gossip about new coaching opportunities or to have the young
sportspersons “pull” gossip about them. Someone argues, “Push gossip is slow.
It is better to do pull gossip, and even with fixed fanout, it converges in
O(log(log(N)) time!” Are they right? If yes, give a proof (informal proof ok). If
they are wrong, give a proof (informal proof). (Note: Push gossip and pull gossip
mentioned here are the same protocols discussed in lecture)

The argument is technically wrong because it is missing a key complexity when it comes to the time required to push/pull in gossip.

In push, only nodes who know the rumor attempt to distribute it. This achieves a doubling O(log N) in the first half (N / 2) of the distribution since a majority of nodes are going to push to nodes that are not infected. However, in the second half of the distribution the distribution most nodes are going to push to nodes that are already infected, so there is no acceleration effect, and big O is still O(log N) in that second half.

In pull, all uninfected nodes ask for the rumor, so that doubling effect of O(log N) is going to occur in the first half of the distribution (N /2) since all infected nodes are likely to get hit with an uninfected node asking for the rumor each heartbeat. There is not an acceleration effect in the first half of the distribution because some uninfected nodes will ping nodes that are also uninfected. But, in the second half of the distribution, all uninfected nodes are very likely to ping a node that is already infected, and this leads to a distribution acceleration effect of O(log(log(N))

Therefore, pull gossip is the better option for this fixed fanout gossip distribution. But, the first half of the distribution (N / 2) is going to have a time complexity of O(log N) and it is only until after half of the nodes have been infected that O(log(log(N)) is achieved.





















Question 7:

a. They use Gossip-style failure detection, but they set T_cleanup = 0
 T_cleanup decides how long after a node is dead (failing heartbeat) that it gets removed. If T_cleanup is equal to 0 then removal latency is low since there is no extra wait but this creates ghosts, where nodes gossip the old heartbeat since they haven’t heard about the failure.

b. They use SWIM-style failure detection, but they removed the Suspicion
feature.
SWIM: heartbeat distribution is Alive → Suspect → dead dissemination pattern. Removing Suspicion then the failure detection will just be failed ping = dead. Cut down on detection times since only one heartbeat period is required to determine dead. Lead to false positives since nodes cannot refute their failed ping.

c. They use SWIM-style failure detection, but they removed the round-robin
pinging + random permutation, and instead just randomly selected each
ping target.
Advantage here is a simpler workflow with less moving parts and failure points in the actual detection method, less overhead. Latency is vanilla SWIM. Disadvantage is that a failure could be missed for an undefined number of heartbeat periods, leading to no guaranteed accuracy.





























